antonal-rebellion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cantonal Rebellion</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1873–1874</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cantonal Rebellion was an uprising of intransigent federal republicans in southern and eastern Spain during the First Spanish Republic.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/canu-heledd"><span class="enc-name">Canu Heledd</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Canu Heledd is a cycle of early Welsh englyn poems, probably composed in the 9th or 10th century, in which the princess Heledd laments the death of her brother…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/canu-llywarch-hen"><span class="enc-name">Canu Llywarch Hen</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Canu Llywarch Hen is a cycle of early Welsh poems in englyn metre, probably composed in the ninth or tenth century and preserved mainly in the Red Book of Hergest.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/canusium"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/canusium.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Canusium</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Canusium, modern Canosa di Puglia, was one of the main towns of the Daunians in northern Apulia, famed for its rich chamber tombs and painted pottery.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/canute-guild"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/canute-guild.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Canute Guild</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Canute Guild was a guild of craftsmen in Reval (Tallinn), named after the Danish saint Canute and dominated by German master artisans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/canute-lavard"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/canute-lavard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Canute Lavard</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Canute Lavard was a Danish prince, son of King Eric I, who became Duke of Schleswig around 1115.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/canute-vi-of-denmark"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/canute-vi-of-denmark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Canute VI of Denmark</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Canute VI was king of Denmark from 1182 to 1202, the son of Valdemar I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang"><span class="enc-name">Cao Bằng</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Bằng is a mountainous province of northern Vietnam on the border with China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cao-cao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cao Cao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Cao was a Chinese warlord and statesman of the late Han period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-huan"><span class="enc-name">Cao Huan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Huan was the last emperor of Cao Wei, reigning from 260 to 266 as a figurehead under the Sima family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-kun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cao-kun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cao Kun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Kun was a Chinese general of the Beiyang Army and a leader of the Zhili clique of warlords.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cao-pi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cao Pi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Pi was the son of the warlord Cao Cao and the first emperor of the Wei dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-shu-zhenduo"><span class="enc-name">Cao Shu Zhenduo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Shu Zhenduo was a son of King Wen of Zhou and younger brother of King Wu, who after the Zhou conquest of Shang in the eleventh century BCE enfeoffed him at Taoqiu…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cao-wei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cao Wei</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Cao Wei, or the Wei dynasty, was the state founded by Cao Pi in 220 CE after he deposed the last Han emperor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/caodaism"><span class="enc-name">Caodaism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Caodaism is a syncretic religion founded in southern Vietnam in 1926, with its Holy See at Tây Ninh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/caonabo"><span class="enc-name">Caonabo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Caonabo was a Taíno cacique of Maguana in central Hispaniola and husband of Anacaona of Jaragua.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cap-malheureux"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cap-malheureux.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cap Malheureux</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cap Malheureux is a cape and village at the northern tip of Mauritius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cap-haitien.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cap-Haïtien</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cap-Haïtien is a port on the north coast of Haiti.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cap-vert"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cap-vert.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cap-Vert</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cap-Vert is the peninsula on the coast of Senegal that forms the westernmost point of mainland Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/caparra"><span class="enc-name">Caparra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Caparra was the first Spanish settlement in Puerto Rico, founded in 1508 by Juan Ponce de León a few kilometres inland from San Juan Bay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-araxos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-araxos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Araxos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Araxos is the north-western tip of the Peloponnese in Greece, in western Achaea, where the Gulf of Patras opens into the Ionian Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador"><span class="enc-name">Cape Bojador</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Bojador is a headland on the Atlantic coast of Western Sahara, south of the Canary Islands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-coast.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Coast</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Coast is a port town on the coast of present-day Ghana, in the lands of the Akan kingdom of Fetu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-coast-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Coast Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Coast Castle is a fort on the coast of Ghana, first built by the Swedes in 1653 as Carolusborg and taken by the English in 1664.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-cod.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Cod</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Cod is a hook-shaped peninsula in south-eastern Massachusetts reaching about 100 kilometres into the Atlantic Ocean.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod-bay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-cod-bay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Cod Bay</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Cod Bay is a large bay of the Atlantic Ocean in Massachusetts, enclosed by the hooked peninsula of Cape Cod.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-colony.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Colony</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cape Colony was the settlement at the Cape of Good Hope established in 1652 by the Dutch East India Company as a resupply station for ships sailing to Asia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-delgado"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-delgado.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Delgado</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Delgado is a headland on the coast of northern Mozambique, close to the border with Tanzania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch"><span class="enc-name">Cape Dutch</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Dutch was the form of Dutch spoken at the Cape of Good Hope by settlers, the enslaved and Khoekhoe communities from the 17th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-guardafui"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-guardafui.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Guardafui</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Guardafui is the headland at the tip of the Horn of Africa in north-eastern Somalia, where the Gulf of Aden meets the Indian Ocean.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-horn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-horn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Horn</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Horn is the southernmost headland of the Tierra del Fuego archipelago in Chile, rounded by Dutch navigators in 1616.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-juby"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cape-juby.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cape Juby</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cape Juby is a headland on the Atlantic coast of southern Morocco, opposite the Canary Islands, beside the town of Tarfaya.</span></a></li></ul>
